## 1 · Aggregate and value objects

**Aggregate root:** `User`. Boundary: the user and their email. `Department` enters by
**reference (id)**, not composition — it's another aggregate, and a user doesn't control
it.

| Field | Type | Why this type |
|---|---|---|
| `id` | `UserId` | Value object over `UUID`. Prevents swapping a user id for an order id in a signature |
| `email` | `Email` | Concentrates format validation in one place. `String` spread it across controller, service, and test |
| `name` | `String` | No rule of its own beyond length, which `User`'s constructor checks |
| `departmentId` | `Optional<DepartmentId>` | Optional in the payload. `Optional` on a query return is allowed; here it's a field — **see note** |

> **Compliance note.** `@.claude/rules/code-quality.md` forbids `Optional` on a field.
> Resolution: the field is `DepartmentId departmentId`, possibly absent, modeled as a
> sealed `UserDepartment` (`Assigned` \| `Unassigned`), or the field is mandatory and the
> default is made explicit. Decided: **mandatory field with `DepartmentId.unassigned()`**
> — no `null`, no `Optional` on a field.

**Value objects**

| VO | Wraps | Own invariant | Factory |
|---|---|---|---|
| `UserId` | `UUID` | not null | `UserId.of(UUID)` · `UserId.newId()` |
| `Email` | `String` | valid format, ≤ 254 characters, normalized to lowercase | `Email.of(String)` |
| `DepartmentId` | `UUID` | not null | `DepartmentId.of(UUID)` · `DepartmentId.unassigned()` |

All `record`, immutable, with validation in the compact constructor and a non-public
constructor. Shape: `templates/ValueObject.java.example`.

## 2 · Invariants

| # | Invariant | Where it's enforced | Exception | `errorCode` |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | `email` has a valid format | `Email` compact constructor | `ValidationException` | `EMAIL_INVALID` |
| 2 | `email` has ≤ 254 characters | `Email` compact constructor | `ValidationException` | `EMAIL_TOO_LONG` |
| 3 | `name` has 2 to 120 characters | `User` compact constructor | `ValidationException` | `USER_NAME_LENGTH` |
| 4 | no required field is null | each record's compact constructor | `ValidationException` | `<FIELD>_REQUIRED` |
| 5 | `email` is unique in the system | **outside the aggregate** — the output port checks, and the constraint arbitrates | `ConflictException` | `EMAIL_ALREADY_USED` |

Invariant 5 doesn't fit in the aggregate: it requires knowing the other users, and an
aggregate doesn't query the repository. It lives in the input port, before building the
`User`, and the `UNIQUE` constraint closes the race — see `20-persistencia.md`.

No generic JDK exception. `@.claude/rules/error-handling.md`.

## 3 · Ports

**Input** — `application/port/in/CreateUserUseCase.java`

```
interface CreateUserUseCase
    UserId create(CreateUserCommand command)
```

One method, one use case. Throws `ValidationException` (invariants 1-4, propagated from
the constructors) and `ConflictException` (invariant 5).

**Command** — `application/port/in/CreateUserCommand.java`

```
record CreateUserCommand(String email, String name, UUID departmentId)
```

Primitive and JDK types at the input: the command is the boundary where the outside
world hasn't been validated yet. It's the implementation that builds the value objects
and lets the invariants fire. Zero framework types — no `jakarta.validation`, which
lives in the adapter's DTO.

**Output** — `application/port/out/UserRepository.java`

```
interface UserRepository
    boolean existsByEmail(Email email)
    User save(User user)
```

`existsByEmail` serves invariant 5. `save` returns the persisted aggregate — the id may
be generated by the database. No JPA, Spring, or SQL types in the signatures.

**Implementation** — `application/service/CreateUserService.java`. Implements
`CreateUserUseCase`. Constructor injection, `private final` fields. This is where the
transaction opens and closes (`@.claude/rules/architecture-ddd.md`, Application section);
the annotation lives on this class, which is application layer, not domain.

## 4 · Events

| Event | Payload | Emitted when | Consumed by |
|---|---|---|---|
| `UserCreated` | `UserId`, `Email`, `Instant occurredAt` | `User` built and persisted successfully | **UC-002** — welcome email |

Past-tense name. Immutable `record`, no framework types. `occurredAt` comes from an
injected `Clock`, never `Instant.now()` — `@.claude/rules/architecture-ddd.md`.

The event is what makes splitting UC-001/UC-002 possible: without it, sending the email
would have to live inside this use case, which is exactly what the mother spec rejected.

Publishing and transport aren't part of this file. Who publishes and with what guarantee
is a `messaging-architect` decision.

